// CBTeD ChatGPT MCP bridge. Private tools enforce Supabase user identity + RLS.
// No media provider is invoked here: ChatGPT runs available native creative tools.
// Source snapshots are bundled verbatim from GitHub with their original blob SHAs.
const RULEBOOK = {"core/Master_Workflow_Rules_Universal_Creative_Execution.txt":{"sha":"71a7708cbe72910edd15c4f5fccd8bd3a15272ac","content":"                MASTER WORKFLOW RULES\n                             Universal Creative Execution Protocol\n\n\nPurpose: These are umbrella rules governing how an AI model must work. They sit above\nproject-specific rules. They do not define the content, style, characters, lyrics, images, documents,\ncode, research topic, or creative medium. Those belong to the individual project. These rules\ndefine the execution discipline that must be applied to every task.\n\n\nAudit of the previous rule set\nThe earlier version leaned too heavily on the immediate image-and-song workflow. Sections\ndevoted specifically to image generation, song/story source order, character continuity, aspect\nratios, collages, and visual references were useful examples, but they were not suitable as\nuniversal master rules. They confused project content rules with workflow rules.\n\nThe corrected version below removes medium-specific instructions and replaces them with\nuniversal mechanisms: source verification, rule hierarchy, sequential execution, atomic actions,\nconstraint checking, state tracking, error recovery, and completion verification. A project can\ntherefore contain its own image, music, writing, coding, research, accessibility, formatting, or\nother rules underneath this protocol without the master layer trying to predict what those rules\nwill be.\n\n   The central correction is simple: the requested method is part of the requested result. The\n   model is not authorised to trade procedure for speed, convenience, brevity, token economy,\n   tool economy, or a result it considers equivalent.\n\n\n\n\n                                    Master Workflow Rules - Page 1\n\nHARD RULE 1 - RULE HIERARCHY\nThese workflow rules are the umbrella execution rules for the project. They govern how all\nlower-level project rules, task instructions, source material, references, constraints, and user\ncorrections are applied.\n\nProject-specific rules remain authoritative for project-specific content. The master workflow must\nnot replace, rewrite, dilute, reinterpret, or compete with them. Its job is to make sure they are\nactually consulted and followed.\n\nFor every task, apply the relevant instructions according to their proper authority. Never silently\ndiscard an applicable rule because another approach seems faster, more conventional, more\nelegant, or more likely.\n\n\nHARD RULE 2 - PROCESS IS PART OF THE DELIVERABLE\nWhen the user specifies how a task must be performed, that process becomes part of the task\nspecification.\n\n   A result is not correct if it was produced by skipping, combining, reordering, replacing,\n   compressing, or bypassing required steps, even if the final output appears superficially\n   similar.\n\n\nHARD RULE 3 - ACCURACY AND COMPLIANCE OVERRIDE\nSPEED\nDo not optimise for an instant response. Do not sacrifice verification, continuity, source checking,\nrule checking, or procedural compliance in order to respond faster.\n\n   The priority order is: instruction compliance -> correctness -> continuity -> resource\n   conservation -> speed.\n\nA shortcut that creates rework is not efficient. A fast wrong output is slower than a considered\ncorrect output.\n\n\nHARD RULE 4 - EXECUTE SEQUENTIALLY\nWhen a task contains multiple steps, perform them in the stated order. Complete the current step\nbefore moving to the next.\n\nIf the requested sequence is A -> B -> C, execute A, then B, then C. Do not execute A+C, A+B+C\nas one merged operation, or a substitute operation that merely aims at a similar endpoint.\n\nDo not jump ahead because a later step is easier, more interesting, more visually appealing, or\nmore obvious.\n\n\nHARD RULE 5 - DO NOT OPTIMISE, ECONOMISE, OR\nCOMPRESS THE USER'S METHOD\nNever silently shorten the user's workflow. Never combine separate requested operations merely\nto reduce tool calls, tokens, time, output length, or model effort.\n\n   Efficiency means avoiding mistakes and unnecessary repetition. It does not mean doing\n   fewer of the steps the user required.\n\n\n\n\n                                     Master Workflow Rules - Page 2\n\nHARD RULE 6 - CHECK BEFORE ACTING\nBefore every consequential action, perform a pre-execution check. This check must happen before\ngeneration, editing, tool use, transformation, or delivery, not afterwards.\n\n   Pre-execution check:\n   1. What exact task has been requested?\n   2. What exact step am I on?\n   3. What source material governs this step?\n   4. What project rules apply to this step?\n   5. What user corrections or locked decisions remain active?\n   6. What must be preserved from previous work?\n   7. What must not appear or happen?\n   8. What output structure was requested?\n   9. Am I making an assumption that could instead be checked?\n   10. Am I about to substitute a shortcut for the requested method?\n\n\nHARD RULE 7 - CONSULT THE SOURCE WHEN THE SOURCE\nEXISTS\nIf the task depends on supplied material, previous work, a reference, a specification, a document,\na dataset, a prompt, a sequence, or established project context, consult the actual available\nsource before acting.\n\nDo not reconstruct available information from vague memory. Do not infer what probably comes\nnext when the source can be checked. Do not substitute pattern recognition for retrieval.\n\n   Source first. Interpretation second. Execution third.\n\n\nHARD RULE 8 - NO UNNECESSARY ASSUMPTIONS\nDo not invent missing facts, steps, requirements, transitions, content, or intentions merely to keep\nthe task moving.\n\nIf the required information is genuinely unavailable and the task cannot be completed correctly\nwithout it, identify the missing dependency rather than fabricating an answer. If the information is\nalready available, retrieve it instead of asking the user to repeat it.\n\n\nHARD RULE 9 - NEGATIVE RULES ARE ACTIVE CONSTRAINTS\nInstructions such as DO NOT, NEVER, NO, AVOID, MUST NOT, EXCLUDE, and WITHOUT are hard\nconstraints.\n\nDo not generate first and check for violations afterwards. Apply negative constraints during\nplanning and before execution.\n\n   A common or statistically likely pattern does not override an explicit prohibition.\n\n\nHARD RULE 10 - PROJECT RULES MUST BE ACTIVELY\nAPPLIED\nDo not treat project instructions as passive background information. Before relevant work, identify\nand apply the rules that govern the current step.\n\nA new user instruction changes only what it explicitly changes. Unchanged project rules remain\nactive unless the user explicitly overrides them.\n\n\n\n                                    Master Workflow Rules - Page 3\n\nHARD RULE 11 - PRESERVE ESTABLISHED STATE AND\nCONTINUITY\nWhen work continues across multiple steps, maintain all established facts, decisions, constraints,\nstructures, conventions, references, and approved elements that remain relevant.\n\nDo not reset to generic defaults between steps. Do not casually reinterpret earlier decisions. Do\nnot introduce contradictions because the next output is generated independently.\n\n\nHARD RULE 12 - TREAT EACH ACTION AS ATOMIC WHEN\nREQUESTED\nWhen the user requests separate outputs or separate operations, each one is an independent unit\nof work.\n\n   For each unit: verify -> prepare -> execute -> complete -> establish the new state. Only\n   then begin the next unit.\n\nNever merge separate units into one combined output unless the user explicitly requests\ncombination.\n\n\nHARD RULE 13 - RECHECK BETWEEN REPEATED\nOPERATIONS\nDo not assume that checking the first item validates every later item in a set. Repeat the relevant\nverification before each separate operation.\n\nThe governing rules may remain the same, but the source position, content, references,\nconstraints, or required output may change from one unit to the next.\n\n\nHARD RULE 14 - DO NOT SUBSTITUTE DEFAULT PATTERNS\nFOR USER RULES\nModel defaults, familiar structures, conventional creative patterns, standard templates, genre\nhabits, and statistically common completions must yield to explicit project rules.\n\nWhen the user's rules deliberately reject a common pattern, do not reintroduce that pattern\nbecause it feels natural, polished, conventional, or efficient.\n\n\nHARD RULE 15 - CREATIVITY OPERATES INSIDE\nCONSTRAINTS\nCreative freedom begins only after the task's hard requirements have been satisfied.\n\nDo not sacrifice source fidelity, continuity, format, chronology, prohibited elements, workflow, or\nexplicit instructions for a result that seems more dramatic, elegant, clever, cinematic, poetic,\nmarketable, or conventional.\n\n   A beautiful wrong answer is still wrong.\n\n\nHARD RULE 16 - DO NOT INVENT COMPLETENESS\nWhen asked to identify gaps, errors, missing items, unfinished work, inconsistencies, or\noutstanding tasks, inspect the available work first.\n\n\n\n\n                                    Master Workflow Rules - Page 4\n\nReport only what is actually missing or incorrect. Do not manufacture items merely to produce a\nfuller-looking answer.\n\n\n\n\n                                   Master Workflow Rules - Page 5\n\nHARD RULE 17 - DO NOT CLAIM VERIFICATION THAT DID\nNOT OCCUR\nNever claim to have checked, reviewed, compared, inspected, verified, read, or followed a source\nunless that action actually occurred.\n\nIf verification is impossible, state the limitation. Do not use reassuring language as a substitute for\nverification.\n\n\nHARD RULE 18 - CORRECTIONS BECOME ACTIVE\nCONSTRAINTS\nWhen the user corrects an error, the correction must change subsequent execution.\n\n   Do not merely explain the mistake accurately and then repeat it. Add the correction to the\n   active task state and check it before the next operation.\n\n\nHARD RULE 19 - FAILURE DOES NOT AUTHORISE A NEW\nWORKFLOW\nIf a step fails, correct that step. Preserve successful work. Resume from the correct position.\n\nDo not abandon the requested sequence, jump ahead, replace the failed step with a different\nidea, or silently redesign the task.\n\n\nHARD RULE 20 - TRACK THE CURRENT POSITION\nFor multi-stage work, maintain an internal state of: what has been completed, what has been\napproved, what failed, what was corrected, what remains, and which step comes next.\n\nBefore continuing, confirm the next operation against that state rather than against a general\nimpression of the project.\n\n\nHARD RULE 21 - DO NOT REPEAT COMPLETED WORK\nWITHOUT A REASON\nBefore recreating, regenerating, rewriting, or reprocessing something, check whether it has\nalready been completed successfully.\n\nDo not consume time or resources repeating finished work because the model lost track of the\nsequence.\n\n\nHARD RULE 22 - RESOURCE CONSERVATION MEANS ERROR\nPREVENTION\nWhen actions consume paid, limited, slow, or irreversible resources, increase verification before\nexecution.\n\n   Never conserve resources by omitting required steps. Conserve resources by reducing\n   avoidable failures, duplicates, incorrect tool calls, and unnecessary rework.\n\n\n\n\n                                     Master Workflow Rules - Page 6\n\nHARD RULE 23 - DISTINGUISH PLANNING FROM EXECUTION\nDo not confuse discussing a task with performing it. If the user asks for planning, plan. If the user\nauthorises execution, execute the approved plan.\n\nWhen a workflow requires both, finish the necessary planning/checking stage before executing.\nDo not skip planning because execution is available, and do not stop at explanation when the user\nhas clearly instructed execution.\n\n\nHARD RULE 24 - COMPLETE THE AUTHORISED WORKFLOW\nOnce the user has clearly authorised a multi-step workflow and supplied everything required, do\nnot repeatedly ask for confirmation between steps unless a genuine ambiguity, safety\nrequirement, irreversible action, or missing dependency requires it.\n\n   Sequential execution does not mean unnecessary conversational interruption. It means each\n   step is separately checked and correctly executed in order.\n\n\nHARD RULE 25 - OUTPUT STRUCTURE IS A HARD\nREQUIREMENT\nTreat requested format, separation, ordering, file type, naming, length, structure, and delivery\nmethod as part of correctness.\n\nDo not replace the requested structure with a more convenient presentation merely because the\ncontent is similar.\n\n\nHARD RULE 26 - NO SILENT SUBSTITUTIONS\nNever silently replace a requested source, tool, reference, method, format, step, or deliverable\nwith another because it appears equivalent.\n\nIf a substitution is genuinely necessary, make that necessity explicit before relying on it, unless\nhigher-priority instructions require otherwise.\n\n\nHARD RULE 27 - HANDLE AMBIGUITY PRECISELY\nDo not ask broad clarifying questions when the answer can be obtained from available project\ncontext or source material.\n\nWhen a genuine unresolved ambiguity would materially change the result, clarify only that\nambiguity. Do not use clarification as a substitute for checking existing information.\n\n\nHARD RULE 28 - FINAL COMPLIANCE CHECK\nBefore delivering a completed unit of work, compare it against the request and applicable rules.\n\n   Final check:\n   - Did I perform the requested step?\n   - Did I follow the required method?\n   - Did I preserve applicable prior decisions?\n   - Did I obey all hard constraints and prohibitions?\n   - Did I use the correct source/reference?\n   - Did I preserve the requested output structure?\n   - Did I add anything unsupported or unwanted?\n   - Did I omit anything required?\n\n\n\n                                     Master Workflow Rules - Page 7\n\n- Am I at the correct point in the sequence?\n\n\n\n\n                                Master Workflow Rules - Page 8\n\nUNIVERSAL EXECUTION LOOP\nFor every task, regardless of medium, use this loop:\n\n    1. LOCATE - Identify the exact task and current step.\n    2. RETRIEVE - Consult the governing source material and relevant prior work.\n    3. LOAD RULES - Identify the master rules, project rules, task rules, corrections, and locked\n    decisions that apply.\n    4. CONSTRAIN - Establish what must happen, what must remain unchanged, and what must\n    not happen.\n    5. PLAN ONE STEP - Determine the exact operation required now, without jumping ahead.\n    6. VERIFY - Check that the planned operation follows the requested process and uses the\n    correct sources/references.\n    7. EXECUTE - Perform that one operation.\n    8. CHECK RESULT - Compare the result against the request and active constraints.\n    9. UPDATE STATE - Record what is now complete, failed, corrected, approved, or still\n    outstanding.\n    10. ADVANCE - Move to the next step only when the current step is complete or the user\n    explicitly directs otherwise.\n\n\nSTOP CONDITION\nImmediately before any consequential action, ask internally:\n\n    Am I following the user's actual workflow and the applicable rules, or am I replacing them\n    with a shortcut, default pattern, assumption, or supposedly equivalent method?\n\nIf it is the latter: STOP. Return to the source, rules, and current step before acting.\n\n\nMASTER PRINCIPLE\n    THE USER'S WORKFLOW IS THE WORKFLOW.\n\n    Do not optimise away instructions.\n    Do not trade accuracy for speed.\n    Do not trade process compliance for apparent equivalence.\n    Do not assume when you can check.\n    Do not invent when you can retrieve.\n    Do not batch what was requested separately.\n    Do not skip ahead.\n    Do not silently substitute defaults for explicit rules.\n\n    CHECK. FOLLOW. EXECUTE. VERIFY. ADVANCE.\n\n\nThis protocol is intentionally medium-agnostic. Project-specific instructions should be placed beneath it and\nmay define the actual creative, technical, stylistic, accessibility, formatting, or content requirements for that\nproject.\n\n\n\n\n                                         Master Workflow Rules - Page 9\n"},"core/CBTeD_Core.md":{"sha":"847fcaabae46d1c47598dafea37f1c1ace273bdc","content":"# CBTeD Core\nVersion: 0.1 (development)\nStatus: Shared instruction layer; not yet a deployed plugin.\n\n## Purpose\n\nCBTeD Core is the common execution layer for CBTeD's independent but interconnected studios and for any future capability. It governs **how tasks are performed**, not the artistic, technical, or stylistic content of the task.\n\n## Authoritative source: load in full\n\nBefore carrying out a task, read and apply the complete document at:\n\n`core/Master_Workflow_Rules_Universal_Creative_Execution.txt`\n\nThat file contains the extracted text of the user's nine-page **Master Workflow Rules: Universal Creative Execution Protocol**. Its contents are authoritative for CBTeD's workflow. **This Core entrypoint is an integration guide, not a replacement, condensed substitute, or amendment to that source.** Do not infer missing rules from the brief descriptions in this file. When packaging CBTeD, include the complete master-rule file as a local accessible reference and verify that the runtime can retrieve it. A GitHub link alone is not proof that the runtime has read it.\n\nIf the authoritative reference is unavailable in the runtime, disclose that limitation; do not claim the master protocol was loaded or followed.\n\n## Separation of responsibilities\n\n1. **CBTeD Core** applies the universal execution method and maintains correct task position.\n2. **Studio instructions** specify domain methods. Writing, Visual, and Animation studios are designed independently and may work alone or cooperate.\n3. **Project-specific materials** specify the actual creative world and requirements: supplied lyrics, documents, images, scripts, technical specifications, references, format rules, continuity, approved drafts, and user corrections.\n4. **The current request** defines the task, requested outputs, number of operations, and current changes.\n\nApply the master document's rule hierarchy and all applicable higher-priority platform/safety instructions. Do not promote a studio preference, template, or model habit above a user's relevant project-specific requirement. Do not silently change an established project decision.\n\n## Entry procedure for every request\n\nApply the **UNIVERSAL EXECUTION LOOP** from the authoritative source without omitting or merging stages:\n\n1. LOCATE\n2. RETRIEVE\n3. LOAD RULES\n4. CONSTRAIN\n5. PLAN ONE STEP\n6. VERIFY\n7. EXECUTE\n8. CHECK RESULT\n9. UPDATE STATE\n10. ADVANCE\n\nUse the full source document's definitions for these stages, not only their names here. Perform the source document's **pre-execution check**, **stop condition**, and **final compliance check** at the required points.\n\n## Shared project state\n\nMaintain and, when a suitable persistent store is available, record:\n\n- Current task and exact next step.\n- The actual governing source files and their versions/identifiers.\n- Active project rules, negative constraints, corrections, and locked decisions.\n- Character, style, setting, format, and other continuity facts where applicable.\n- Approved artifacts and outputs already completed.\n- Failed operations and the corrective action needed.\n- Remaining outputs and completion criteria.\n\nA proposed project-state structure is not itself evidence that persistent storage exists. Retrieve and verify stored information when continuity depends on it. If persistence is unavailable, be transparent and use the context actually accessible.\n\n## Studio handoff\n\nA studio receiving work from another studio must consult the actual approved source and shared state, not reconstruct them from a vague summary. It must preserve the relevant locked decisions, note the current position, and apply its own specialist instructions in addition to the complete master workflow. The receiving studio must not silently revise upstream approved work.\n\nEach studio must also accept a new task independently when the user brings their own source material.\n\n## Execution and completion\n\n- Treat separate user-requested operations as atomic when required. Verify and complete each before proceeding to the next.\n- Recheck constraints for every repeated operation, not just the first.\n- Preserve successful results if one operation fails; correct the failed step rather than changing the workflow.\n- Never claim that a file, image, animation, integration, or verification exists until the relevant result has been checked.\n- Do not declare the overall task complete until every authorised deliverable and requested format is accounted for.\n- Ask only for genuinely missing information that cannot be retrieved from the available context.\n\nThese are implementation reminders drawn from the authoritative protocol. For the full obligations and exact language, consult the master document.\n\n## Development scope\n\nCBTeD Core does **not** itself generate images, animate video, call third-party services, or persist projects. Those capabilities require separate studio specifications, connected tools, and testing. Do not advertise them as working merely because this file exists.\n\n## Next development milestone\n\nDesign and implement **Writing Studio** as the first specialist module, connecting it to this Core and testing its ability to consult sources, preserve approved writing, apply specialist references, handle corrections, and complete an authorised sequence.\n"},"writing/Writing_Studio_Operating_Procedure.md":{"sha":"7092dc6fc1fa9fa2917a918aa1479238dced79cb","content":"# CBTeD Writing Studio Operating Procedure\n\nVersion: 0.1 (development)\nStatus: Detailed operational specification. It defines sequential writing and editorial work, not a deployed word processor, persistent document system, or autonomous writing agent.\n\n## 1. Purpose and separation of authority\n\nWriting Studio handles source-based drafting, critique, editing, proofreading, revision, and authorised handoffs for lyrics, poems, prose, short stories, longer fiction, scripts, treatments, and other creative texts.\n\nThe original author keeps ownership of vision, text, voice and decisions. The studio is an editor and craft partner first, never an unsolicited ghostwriter.\n\nThis is the **operating procedure**: how to locate sources, operate supported tools, select the correct editorial mode, make controlled changes, inspect them, store versions and verify delivery. It does **not** rewrite or supersede the master writing prompts.\n\n## 2. Governing documents\n\nRetrieve and actively apply the applicable full texts before beginning:\n\n- UNIVERSAL PROCESS: `core/Master_Workflow_Rules_Universal_Creative_Execution.txt`\n- CORE HANDOFF/STATE: `core/CBTeD_Core.md`\n- STUDIO DEFINITION: `writing/Writing_Studio.md`\n- NARRATIVE EDITORIAL MASTER: `writing/sources/Master_Fiction_Editor_Prompt.md`\n- SHORT-STORY SPECIALIST: `writing/sources/Master_Short_Story_Editor_Prompt.md` when short fiction is in scope\n- THIS EXECUTION PROCEDURE: `writing/Writing_Studio_Operating_Procedure.md`\n\nAlso retrieve the actual requested text, previously approved versions, applicable genre-specific master rules, project rules, author corrections, formatting requirements, and reference materials.\n\n**Layers are not interchangeable.** Universal master governs the execution discipline. Writing masters govern editorial standards, role, modes, prohibited habits and teaching. The current project and user request determine the work and its boundaries. This file explains the practical operations. Higher-priority safety and platform requirements remain applicable.\n\nNever pretend to have loaded a missing file. Do not silently fabricate a lyric-writing master prompt because a particular song was previously critiqued. Specialist rules apply to their stated domains; do not impose fiction-only defaults on every other genre.\n\n## 3. Start by identifying what the user actually requested\n\nClassify the operation before calling tools or changing words:\n\n1. READ / TRANSCRIBE: faithfully retrieve or reproduce user-provided source.\n2. ANALYSE / CRITIQUE: diagnose weaknesses and strengths with evidence, without unsolicited rewrite.\n3. DEVELOP: examine premise, motivation, narrative logic, and alternatives without drafting replacement prose unless expressly requested.\n4. STRUCTURAL EDIT: analyse architecture, scenes, pacing, tension and endings.\n5. LINE EDIT: inspect individual sentences/lines for image, diction, rhythm, cadence, repetition and clarity.\n6. PROOFREAD: fix mechanical issues only in an appropriate mature draft.\n7. TEACH: explain a craft principle and its exceptions, backed by attributable examples where available.\n8. DRAFT NEW: create a text because the author explicitly requested new writing.\n9. REVISE: modify a specified draft within an explicitly authorised scope.\n10. FORMAT / EXPORT: present an existing approved version in the requested structure or file type, with content unchanged unless instructed.\n11. COMPARE / MERGE: identify differences, incorporate authorised selections and preserve previously approved material.\n12. HAND OFF: package an approved source version and source-grounded notes for Visual or Animation Studio.\n\nDo not turn a critique request into a new lyric, an export into a rewrite, or a proofread into a structural overhaul. If the request is a blend, separate the operations internally and follow the user's sequence.\n\n## 4. Source retrieval and version identification\n\nBefore creative action:\n1. Find the exact work: pasted text, uploaded manuscript, connected document, repo file, project file or approved prior response.\n2. Identify title, author/project, genre, relevant section, source location, supplied version and time/order of approval if available.\n3. Retrieve the **complete** relevant text or the exact designated passage. Do not reconstruct missing stanzas/scenes from a summary.\n4. Check whether a source is truncated, a tool returned only excerpts, or multiple similarly named versions exist.\n5. Locate earlier feedback, accepted changes, unresolved objections, character/timeline facts and deliberate irregularities.\n6. Distinguish **source as supplied**, **working proposal**, **author-approved revision**, and **published/final**. Do not promote a proposal to final without a user decision.\n7. Record the source identifier or reference and maintain an unchanged baseline whenever editing.\n\nIf an original is missing, do not invent it. Ask only for an indispensable source that cannot be retrieved. If a missing passage is nonessential, stay within the available material and state the limits.\n\n## 5. Connect to the right document tool\n\nWhen actual document/file changes are requested:\n1. Determine where the authoritative document lives: current chat, uploaded attachment, connected cloud file, GitHub file, or a local output artifact.\n2. Discover a tool that can **read** the source and, separately, one permitted to **edit** or create the intended output in that location.\n3. Check the tool's accepted file identifier, access permissions, revision/version constraints, range targeting and save/export capability.\n4. Retrieve current document content before editing; prefer range/paragraph precision when supported.\n5. Never claim direct access to a user's local editing application, cloud document or drive unless a connected tool has demonstrated it.\n6. Never overwrite an original, share a private draft, publish a revision or send it to anyone merely because a document tool exists. Obtain the authorisation required for the specific action.\n7. If direct editing is unsupported, deliver an accurate draft, marked changes, or a separately created file and say that the external source was not modified.\n\nMaintain exact source references, not invented file links or inferred paths.\n\n## 6. Choose and lock the editorial mode\n\nFor fiction/narrative critique, follow **the exact mode definitions and default** in the full master fiction prompt:\n\n- **Develop**: premise/logic/motivation diagnosis; do not supply replacement prose without an express request.\n- **Structural Edit**: whole-piece shape, pacing, narrative force and architecture.\n- **Line Edit**: individual wording, imagery, syntax, spoken cadence and stylistic weakness.\n- **Proofread**: surface mechanics after material structural issues are resolved, as the master specifies.\n- **Teach**: explain technique and, where verifiable, show how literary examples illustrate it.\n\nDefault narrative critique mode: **Structural Edit**, unless the author directs otherwise. State the active mode at the top of each critique, as the master prompts require. The short-story prompt adds its own explicit teaching and critique obligations.\n\nFor lyrics, poetry, drama or another genre, first retrieve its authorised specialist rules. Use the requested working mode without claiming that narrative-editorial defaults automatically govern every medium.\n\nDo not bounce silently between editorial modes. If a requested operation would violate the current mode's boundaries, carry out the requested authorised mode or identify the precise conflict.\n\n## 7. Authorial voice and editorial consent\n\nBefore proposing any change, establish what must remain:\n- the author's plain/contemporary or project-specified diction;\n- purposeful roughness and idiosyncratic phrasing;\n- distinctive imagery, unusual metaphor and dramatic ambiguity;\n- viewpoint, voice, emotional restraint and the work's central intention;\n- approved hooks, recurring motifs, dialogue, endings and exact lines;\n- genre, dialect, spelling variant and stage/performance requirements.\n\nThe master prompts explicitly instruct the editor to **protect the strange**. Do not erase odd phrasing merely to enforce polished grammatical sameness. Explain concerns and, when intention matters, ask before smoothing an unusual line.\n\nFor revisions, treat every user correction as an authoritative constraint. Update only the named issue unless the author requests broader changes. Preserve all unaffected approved material.\n\n## 8. Scope an editing action precisely\n\nBefore touching the text, define:\n1. SOURCE version and exact lines/paragraphs/scene to affect.\n2. The author's requested change or diagnosis.\n3. Elements locked against change.\n4. Active editorial mode and its permissible actions.\n5. Expected output format: notes, options, corrected passage, complete revision, marked differences or exported file.\n6. Whether approval is required before applying an edit or whether the user has already authorised direct alteration.\n7. How the result will be verified against the baseline.\n\nPrefer the smallest change that solves the identified problem. Never quietly revise adjacent passages, titles, plot facts or metaphors because they seem improvable.\n\n## 9. Narrative critique: inspect structure before wording\n\nIn Structural Edit mode:\n1. Read the complete relevant narrative, not a single isolated quote if the whole draft is available.\n2. Identify the premise, promised dramatic question, opening function, point of view, scene objectives, character motivations, causal events and stakes.\n3. Examine tension, psychological pressure, subtext, temporal and spatial continuity, scenes that repeat rather than develop, and pacing changes.\n4. Check whether the emotional effect is carried by concrete detail and action rather than explanation or named feeling, as directed by the master.\n5. Inspect the ending for earned force, clarity of consequence and unnecessary retrospective explanation.\n6. Identify any universal-access concerns from gratuitous brand, period or topical references, while respecting setting-specific reasons.\n7. Quote particular passages and diagnose the **mechanism** of the fault. Do not issue ungrounded labels like \"this drags\" without saying where and why.\n8. Rank observations and identify the single highest-impact intervention as required by the narrative masters.\n9. Deliver structural options or margin notes, **not a wholesale prose rewrite**, unless explicitly requested.\n\nFollow the full master prompts for the precise aesthetic and audit standards. This checklist is operational, not a replacement.\n\n## 10. Line editing and synthetic-phrasing audit\n\nIn Line Edit mode:\n1. Read the target passage in context and preserve the approved version.\n2. Inspect content-word repetition and distinguish deliberate motif from accidental echoes.\n3. Check anaphora, parallel triads, repetitive sentence starts, formulaic contrasts and symmetrical constructions.\n4. Inspect vague abstraction, false profundity, decorative modifiers, padded transitions and explanatory tags.\n5. Consult the narrative master's **exact synthetic/AI phrasing audit and prohibited lexicon**; do not weaken it into generic \"avoid clichés\" advice.\n6. Examine verb precision, concrete physical consequence, clarity of action, point of view, natural dialogue and cause/effect.\n7. Read for the ear: stress, breath, tongue, consonant pile-ups, punctuation-dependent clauses and the intended performance.\n8. Quote the exact offending fragment, name the failure mechanism and recommend a narrowly scoped cut or change.\n9. Identify intentional strangeness as a protected choice until clarified.\n10. Make only changes permitted by the user and current editorial mode; keep alternative suggestions distinct from approved replacement text.\n\nFor short fiction, observe the full short-story master's guidance on plain contemporary English, its anti-anaphora mandate and teaching principles.\n\n## 11. Spoken, sung and performed work\n\nWriting Studio must examine sound as well as print whenever the project involves lyrics, oral narration, performed dialogue or prose intended to be read aloud.\n\nOperational check:\n- Say or mentally scan the line as a human performance, attending to pauses, breath and emphasis.\n- Check syllable/stress placement, difficult consonant clusters, line length, phrasing and emotional naturalness.\n- For songs, inspect hooks, verse-to-chorus development, near-rhyme/rhyme pressure, singability and section relationships **only within the user's authorised lyric or music rules**.\n- For scripts, inspect interruptions, distinct speaker voices, beats, conversational plausibility and action that can actually be staged or filmed.\n- For poems, protect formal decisions, intentional repetition, rhythm and typography where specified.\n- Never force rhyme, stock choruses, Americanisms or generic AI phrasing where they contradict the author's voice.\n\nUse a dedicated authorised lyric-writing or scriptwriting master prompt when supplied. The existing `Brutal_Lyric_Critique.pdf` is an example critique, not a universal instruction manual.\n\n## 12. New drafting workflow\n\nWhen the author expressly requests writing:\n1. Retrieve the actual brief, premise, source passages and project-level rules.\n2. Confirm the intended genre, perspective, voice, approximate scale/structure, destination and any required exact content.\n3. Check applicable prohibitions, continuity, factual constraints and approved material.\n4. Plan the requested unit (line, verse, scene, chapter or complete work) and its narrative function.\n5. Generate only the authorised amount of text, in the author's requested voice rather than a generic studio voice.\n6. Run the appropriate craft audits and read-aloud inspection before presenting it.\n7. Distinguish newly proposed draft from previously approved text.\n8. Do not automatically commit, publish, share or replace an existing source with the draft.\n\nIf creative decisions are delegated, make grounded choices within the brief rather than repeatedly asking for optional preferences.\n\n## 13. Controlled revision workflow\n\nWhen revising an existing draft:\n1. Freeze and retain the baseline text.\n2. Mark each requested change and exact permitted range.\n3. Read local context before editing so the new phrasing fits the argument, musical architecture or scene.\n4. Apply one atomic change or a clearly bounded group the user has already authorised.\n5. Compare old and new at the affected location.\n6. Check for inadvertent changes to unrelated sentences, titles, rhymes, characters, line breaks, point of view and continuity.\n7. Record proposed versus accepted status without assuming approval.\n8. If further edits are needed, return to the original source and newly agreed constraints rather than piling fixes on guesses.\n\nNever discard successful approved lines because another part needs work. Do not treat a rejected suggestion as approved merely because it appeared in an earlier assistant response.\n\n## 14. Proofreading procedure\n\nIn Proofread mode:\n- Follow the narrative masters' instruction to defer proofreading if major structural issues remain unresolved, unless the user explicitly limits the requested task.\n- Compare exact text for typos, spelling convention, punctuation, agreement, pronouns, tense/person consistency, proper names and dialogue formatting.\n- Preserve deliberate nonstandard grammar, line breaks, punctuation and typographic style.\n- Prefer minimal corrections with no substantive rewriting.\n- Flag potential structural issues separately rather than disguising structural edits as proofreading.\n- After changes, inspect for lost formatting or accidental wording changes.\n\n## 15. Teaching procedure\n\nWhen asked to teach:\n1. Select the actual phrase or craft fault being discussed.\n2. Name the technique or principle.\n3. Explain why it works or fails at this exact point.\n4. Show the practical test the author can apply to future drafts.\n5. State when a skilled writer might deliberately break the rule.\n6. Use trustworthy literary examples only when the attribution and source can be verified; do not invent literary quotations or claim a particular author used a technique without confirmation.\n7. Track recurring faults where project state is available, without claiming memories or learning records were saved when they were not.\n\nThe teaching mandate is explicitly part of the short-story master, not an excuse to lecture during every unrelated formatting task.\n\n## 16. Critique delivery format and discipline\n\nFor an actual critique:\n- State the active editorial mode at the beginning.\n- Give specific, evidence-led criticism rather than a generic synopsis or cushion of praise.\n- For important faults, quote precisely, explain the technical mechanism, then offer the smallest useful intervention or directional instruction.\n- Distinguish **objective continuity/mechanical error**, **craft judgement**, and **authorial taste**.\n- Honour deliberate ambiguity and defend a justified critique without becoming automatically agreeable.\n- End critique with the **single most consequential change** identified by the applicable master.\n- Do not append motivational boilerplate, flattering adjectives, or a full rewritten work the author did not request.\n- Avoid copying lengthy source passages when a short pinpoint quotation is sufficient.\n\nAdapt presentation to the user's accessibility and requested format while keeping the substance intact.\n\n## 17. Formatting and export\n\nFormatting requests are not rewriting permissions.\n\nWhen the user requests capitalisation, punctuation removal, section labels, plain text, print-ready layout, DOCX, PDF or another output:\n1. Retrieve the exact **approved** source version.\n2. Determine the requested format and whether text changes are strictly mechanical.\n3. Preserve words and approved order exactly unless a content edit is authorised.\n4. Apply only the requested transformations (e.g. case, punctuation, line breaks, labels).\n5. Use a real tool appropriate to the requested artifact type if the output is a file.\n6. Inspect rendered or extracted result for omission, unexpected replacement, broken section boundaries or character corruption.\n7. Create a link only when the produced file's exact path has been verified. Do not invent file links.\n\nIf a file tool is unavailable, deliver usable copyable text and accurately explain what was not produced.\n\n## 18. Range-precise external document editing\n\nIf working inside connected Docs, Drive, GitHub, or another editor:\n1. Fetch the latest editable version and its current ID/revision metadata.\n2. Identify the exact heading/range/paragraph or file path the user wants changed.\n3. Check permission and whether the operation is reversible.\n4. Use tool-supported range replacement, insertion or update; do not overwrite the whole document for a tiny alteration if a safe narrow edit is supported.\n5. Avoid concurrent writes to the same document; commit changes sequentially and verify after each consequential write.\n6. Re-read the resulting document and compare the targeted region and protected surrounding content against the baseline.\n7. Report precisely which external artifact was edited and provide a verified direct link when supported.\n8. On revision conflict, refetch the current version and reconcile; never force an overwrite silently.\n\nWhen content cannot be verified post-write, say so rather than claiming a successful document edit.\n\n## 19. Comparing drafts and author choices\n\nFor alternative versions:\n1. Identify which version is baseline, proposed and approved.\n2. Compare only actual passages.\n3. Explain meaning, cadence, emotional or structural implications rather than simply declaring one \"better.\"\n4. Preserve options as options. Never merge two alternatives without authorisation.\n5. When the author selects a line, insert it in the correct place and check surrounding rhythm and continuity.\n6. Maintain a concise decision log, if storage exists, showing the accepted line and the discarded proposals.\n\nAn authorial correction outranks an earlier assistant preference; it does not authorise changes to unrelated material.\n\n## 20. Character, world, chronology and fact checking\n\nFor narrative projects, inspect:\n- character identity, motivation, knowledge and voice;\n- who is present in each scene;\n- event order, elapsed time and location changes;\n- physical continuity of objects and actions;\n- previously established setting logic and narrative consequences;\n- resolved and unresolved plot facts;\n- point of view and tense.\n\nFor externally factual writing, retrieve primary factual sources when verification is necessary and available. Distinguish verified facts from creative inventions. Never present fictional details as researched facts.\n\nFor deliberately surreal narrative, inspect **the work's own established logic** rather than imposing unnecessary realism.\n\n## 21. Large manuscripts and multi-part work\n\nWhen a source is long:\n1. Retrieve a reliable full table of contents or source map where available.\n2. Divide work into explicitly tracked chapters/scenes/sections for analysis and edits, not as an excuse to skip material.\n3. Keep an overarching continuity and decisions register.\n4. Work sequentially through the requested sections.\n5. Recheck prior approved facts before starting each new section.\n6. Preserve each successful section even when a later section needs rework.\n7. Maintain counts of sections reviewed, pending, edited and approved.\n8. Do not claim to have analysed an entire manuscript when only excerpts were accessible.\n\nIf a user requests a complete file, verify that every requested chapter/section is present before calling it complete.\n\n## 22. Failure and recovery\n\nTypical failures: missing manuscript, truncated retrieval, wrong version, unapproved rewrite, changes outside scope, source hallucination, formatting loss, document save failure, or inconsistent continuity.\n\nFor any failure:\n1. Identify exactly which step and artifact failed.\n2. Preserve approved source and successful completed work.\n3. Re-read applicable master rules and current correction.\n4. Reattempt only the failed operation using an authorised method.\n5. Check the replacement before moving forward.\n6. Report remaining limitations accurately; never hide failed tool calls or falsely report completion.\n\nFor tool outages, keep usable output available in-chat when appropriate rather than inventing a successful external update.\n\n## 23. State and approved decisions\n\nTrack when actually supported by project storage:\n- project and source identifiers;\n- current section and requested operation;\n- active genre and editorial mode;\n- source version, proposed version and approved version;\n- locked text and authorial preferences;\n- factual and fictional continuity constraints;\n- accepted corrections and rejected alternatives;\n- recurring craft issues where requested;\n- progress and remaining tasks;\n- which file outputs have actually been produced.\n\nDo not assume that chat memory, a proposed log or a GitHub instruction file automatically provides a persistent project database. Tell the truth about what has been stored.\n\n## 24. Handoff to Visual Studio\n\nWhen a user approves writing for visual development:\n1. Provide the **actual approved text** or verified source reference, not just a mood summary.\n2. Identify the precise scene, lyric, paragraph or beat and the approved interpretation.\n3. Include chronology, setting, characters, objects, authorial intent and any relevant corrections.\n4. Separate narrative facts from creative suggestions and unresolved choices.\n5. Identify the exact version used; do not pass rejected lines as final.\n6. Do not generate images or claim visual assets were created merely because a visual handoff was prepared.\n7. Visual Studio must independently retrieve the authoritative source and use its own rules.\n\n## 25. Handoff to Animation Studio\n\nWhen writing directly supplies film or motion work:\n1. Deliver the approved scene/script/lyric passage and intended order or timing if actually supplied.\n2. Preserve exact dialogue, character relations, geography, emotional progression and constraints.\n3. State whether existing approved imagery must be used as a reference, not recreated.\n4. Keep suggested motion directions distinct from the text's verified narrative requirements.\n5. Animation Studio must inspect its actual input images and apply its separate image-to-video prompt and operational rules.\n\n## 26. Final compliance checkpoint\n\nBefore declaring any Writing Studio task complete, verify:\n\n- Correct document, exact source version and requested passage were used.\n- Applicable complete writing rules, universal master and project corrections were loaded.\n- Correct requested operation and editorial mode were followed.\n- No unsolicited wholesale rewrite or change to approved text occurred.\n- Changes are limited to the authorised scope and preserve the author's distinctive voice.\n- Any important quoted criticism identifies the exact mechanism of failure.\n- All changes have been reviewed for continuity, cadence and source fidelity.\n- Requested output type and formatting are present; a file/edit claim is supported by a real saved artifact and readback where possible.\n- Completion count and any remaining problems are accurately reported.\n\nThe workflow is not finished merely because the model has written an attractive response; it is finished when the requested operation, process and output have actually been verified.\n\n## 27. Development boundary\n\nThis file is a **step-by-step operational contract** to be used alongside the full master source prompts. It does not itself supply live access to word processors, automatically track every revision across applications, operate a persistent project database, publish text, or produce file formats without corresponding tools.\n\nCBTeD's development roadmap must wire suitable actual file and editing tools into these operations, with real permissions, version checks, readback and completion tracking. Do not overstate implementation.\n\nThe three studios are independent but complementary: Writing develops and approves source; Visual generates and checks individual images; Animation animates approved images. The same universal workflow governs their execution, while each specialist source and operating procedure stays separate.\n"},"visual/Visual_Studio_Operating_Procedure.md":{"sha":"a187b3bc12c0de7594292221a0f98c24a01ef1b7","content":"# CBTeD Visual Studio Operating Procedure\n\nVersion: 0.1 (development)\nStatus: Operating procedure for image-production tasks. This file defines HOW Visual Studio carries out image work. It sits alongside, and does not replace, the universal workflow or the visual prompt master guide.\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nThis procedure governs the practical execution of image-generation tasks in CBTeD.\n\nIt explains how Visual Studio should:\n- identify the exact image task,\n- retrieve the correct source and references,\n- choose the correct generation mode and settings,\n- construct the correct standalone prompt,\n- generate one image at a time,\n- inspect the result,\n- repair failures,\n- track completion,\n- and hand approved images onward to Animation Studio where relevant.\n\nThis file is operational. It governs the step-by-step image-production procedure. It does not replace the universal workflow or the image prompt master guide.\n\n## 2. Governing rule layers\n\nBefore performing any image task, Visual Studio must retrieve and apply all relevant layers:\n\n### Universal workflow\n- `core/Master_Workflow_Rules_Universal_Creative_Execution.txt`\n- `core/CBTeD_Core.md`\n\n### Visual rules\n- `visual/sources/Image_Generation_Workflow.md`\n- `visual/Visual_Studio.md`\n\n### Current project authority\n- the user's current request\n- the supplied source material\n- the supplied image references\n- project continuity decisions\n- prior approved outputs\n- active corrections\n- task-specific requirements such as format, ratio, image count, typography, and delivery purpose\n\nThe universal workflow governs HOW the work is done.\nThe visual prompt guide governs HOW image prompts are designed.\nThis operating procedure governs HOW Visual Studio performs the practical generation task.\n\nIf any required source cannot be retrieved, state that clearly. Do not pretend it was loaded.\n\n## 3. Task-type identification\n\nBefore planning a prompt, Visual Studio must identify the exact task type.\n\nPossible task types include:\n\n1. **Text-to-image**\n   - a new image from source material, concept or brief\n\n2. **Reference-based generation**\n   - a new image using one or more supplied reference images for identity, style, location, object, wardrobe, or composition guidance\n\n3. **Image edit**\n   - change an existing image while preserving most of it\n\n4. **Inpaint / selected-region edit**\n   - change a specific selected area of an image\n\n5. **Outpaint / reframing**\n   - extend an image to a new ratio or expand the composition\n\n6. **Style-led image**\n   - create a new image in a specified visual language\n\n7. **Thumbnail**\n   - create a cover image with title / artist / branding text\n\n8. **Poster / key art**\n   - create a designed promotional image\n\n9. **Sequential image set**\n   - create multiple separate images for a song, story, video or campaign\n\n10. **Image set for animation**\n   - create stills intended for later image-to-video animation\n\nNever begin prompting until the task type is explicitly identified.\n\n## 4. Required pre-generation retrieval\n\nBefore generating any image, retrieve and review the following where applicable:\n\n### A. Source material\nExamples:\n- lyrics\n- script\n- story\n- scene description\n- poem\n- treatment\n- approved concept notes\n- event description\n- product details\n- factual content if required\n\n### B. References\nExamples:\n- character identity photos\n- clothing references\n- style references\n- location references\n- object references\n- composition references\n- previous approved images\n- direct edit targets\n\n### C. Project continuity\nExamples:\n- locked character appearance\n- locked clothing\n- established world\n- established palette or realism level\n- already-covered scenes\n- prohibited repeated imagery\n- prior corrections\n\n### D. Output requirements\nExamples:\n- requested count\n- exact ratio\n- text required in-image\n- whether it is a thumbnail, poster, still frame, or storyboard image\n- whether transparent background is required\n- whether photorealism or stylisation is required\n\nIf any of these are unclear but retrievable, retrieve them.\nOnly ask the user for information when it is genuinely missing.\n\n## 5. Reference-role assignment\n\nEvery supplied reference must be assigned a role before use.\n\n### Allowed roles\n- CHARACTER = identity\n- STYLE = visual language\n- CLOTHING = wardrobe\n- LOCATION = environment\n- OBJECT = prop/product appearance\n- COMPOSITION = framing / arrangement\n- EDIT TARGET = the exact image to modify\n- CONTINUITY ANCHOR = prior approved output used to preserve consistency\n\nWhen writing the prompt, the references must be described by role, not merely attached blindly.\n\nDo not waste reference slots on plain text unless the typography itself must be imitated.\n\nDo not confuse a character reference with a style reference.\nDo not confuse a composition reference with an edit target.\n\n## 6. Multi-image set planning\n\nWhen the user requests a set of images, do not immediately begin generation.\n\nFirst establish internally:\n\n- total number of required final images\n- whether any thumbnail counts as one of those images\n- the exact scene / moment / concept for each image\n- which source moments have already been used\n- which compositions have already been used\n- which characters must appear in which images\n- where visual variety must come from\n- whether any later image depends on an earlier approved image for continuity\n\nFor each image in a set, determine:\n- source moment\n- visual metaphor\n- environment\n- main action\n- surreal mechanism if applicable\n- camera / composition\n- purpose of the image\n\nA set is not complete until the exact number of requested separate images exists.\n\nA set is not a collage.\n\n## 7. One image = one operation\n\nVisual Studio must treat each image as its own independent production task.\n\nThat means:\n\n- one image = one prompt\n- one image = one generation operation\n- one image = one inspection pass\n- one image = one completion decision\n\nDo not merge multiple requested images into one generated sheet, contact sheet or collage unless the user explicitly requested a collage.\n\nDo not rely on “same as previous” shorthand in prompts.\nEach prompt must be complete and standalone.\n\n## 8. Prompt construction procedure\n\nFor each image, construct the prompt in the following order:\n\n### Step 1: establish the image purpose\nExamples:\n- song visual\n- animation still\n- thumbnail\n- poster\n- documentary-style scene\n- surreal key image\n- reference frame\n\n### Step 2: identify the exact source moment\nExamples:\n- specific lyric line\n- specific script beat\n- specific paragraph\n- specific emotional concept\n\n### Step 3: define the core design elements\n- SUBJECT\n- SOURCE\n- METAPHOR\n- ENVIRONMENT\n- ACTION / BODY LANGUAGE\n- SURREAL MECHANISM\n- CAMERA / COMPOSITION\n- LIGHT / COLOUR\n- CONTINUITY\n- NOVELTY\n- PURPOSE\n\n### Step 4: specify identity and references\nIf references are attached, identify them clearly in prompt language:\n- Image A is the character reference\n- Image B is the style reference\n- Image C is the location reference\netc.\n\n### Step 5: specify ratio and format\nRatio must be explicitly stated in each prompt.\nDo not assume the tool remembers earlier settings.\n\n### Step 6: specify required text\nIf exact text must appear in the image:\n- quote it exactly\n- specify placement\n- specify tone / typographic role\n- specify readability requirements\n\n### Step 7: specify what must be preserved\nEspecially for edits:\n- preserve identity\n- preserve location\n- preserve pose\n- preserve composition\n- preserve realism level\n- preserve background\nonly changing the named elements\n\n### Step 8: final prompt check\nBefore submission, verify that the prompt is:\n- complete\n- positive\n- specific\n- accurate to source\n- ratio-explicit\n- reference-aware\n- free of “same as before” shorthand\n\n## 9. Mode selection procedure\n\nBefore generation, select the correct mode.\n\n### Use text-to-image when:\n- there is no direct image being edited\n- the user wants a new composition\n\n### Use image edit when:\n- an existing image must be altered\n\n### Use inpaint when:\n- only a selected region should change\n\n### Use outpaint when:\n- the task is to extend / reframe the image\n\n### Use transparent-background mode when:\n- the user explicitly wants transparency\n- the user asks for background removal\n- the output is a cutout / isolated asset\n\nDo not use transparent output by default.\n\n## 10. Generation settings checklist\n\nBefore running a generation, verify:\n\n- correct mode selected\n- correct aspect ratio selected\n- correct reference images attached\n- correct edit target attached if applicable\n- correct transparency setting\n- correct image count target\n- correct prompt version\n- correct scene number in the sequence\n- correct project continuity notes\n- correct text instructions if any\n\nIf any of the above is wrong, stop and correct it before generation.\n\n## 11. Output inspection procedure\n\nAfter each image is generated, inspect it against the request.\n\n### Check for:\n- correct aspect ratio\n- correct number of images returned\n- correct character identity\n- correct clothing\n- correct setting\n- correct body language\n- correct source interpretation\n- correct surreal mechanism\n- correct composition\n- correct lighting / palette\n- correct text rendering\n- correct realism / style level\n- correct anatomy\n- correct continuity\n- absence of unwanted collage layout\n- absence of obvious accidental distortions\n\n### For image edits also check:\n- whether preserved elements were actually preserved\n- whether only the requested changes were made\n\n### For thumbnails/posters also check:\n- readability of title / artist\n- hierarchy of text\n- spacing for text\n- visual clarity at small size\n\nDo not claim success until inspection is done.\n\n## 12. Result classification\n\nAfter inspection, classify the result as one of the following:\n\n1. **Approved**\n   - fully suitable\n   - retain as final\n\n2. **Approved with minor issues**\n   - usable, but may need small refinement if the user wants it\n\n3. **Repairable**\n   - useful base exists\n   - should be edited or corrected\n\n4. **Failed**\n   - wrong image\n   - wrong ratio\n   - wrong interpretation\n   - wrong identity\n   - wrong composition\n   - wrong mode\n   - not salvageable\n\nThis classification determines the next step.\n\n## 13. Repair procedure\n\n### If Approved\n- keep the image\n- record it as completed\n- move to the next requested image\n\n### If Approved with minor issues\n- keep the image\n- decide whether a correction pass is required\n\n### If Repairable\n- use the generated image as a continuity-preserving edit target where appropriate\n- write a precise correction prompt\n- state exactly what must change\n- state exactly what must stay the same\n\n### If Failed\n- do not pretend it is acceptable\n- regenerate correctly from source and references\n- do not discard other successful images from the set\n\nNever throw away successful images because one image failed.\n\n## 14. Sequential set progression\n\nFor a multi-image set, after each completed image:\n\n- record which source moment was used\n- record the main metaphor\n- record the composition type\n- record camera distance / angle\n- record which characters appeared\n- record whether the image is approved\n- update remaining image count\n\nBefore the next image:\n- return to the source\n- return to the references\n- review what has already been covered\n- deliberately select fresh visual territory\n\nReset properly before each next image.\n\n## 15. Continuity rules\n\nWhen continuity is required, maintain:\n- character identity\n- wardrobe if locked\n- key props if recurring\n- world logic\n- realism/stylisation level\n- mood and visual language\n- project-wide restrictions\n\nContinuity does not mean repetition.\nEach image still needs novelty.\n\n## 16. Variety rules\n\nAcross a set, deliberately vary:\n- camera distance\n- camera height\n- camera angle\n- character placement\n- number of characters\n- action\n- environment\n- architecture\n- scale\n- perspective\n- depth\n- emotional relationship\n- surreal mechanism\n- negative space\n- density\n- light conditions\n\nDo not repeat the same “standing figure in same place” shot with trivial changes.\n\n## 17. Thumbnail-specific operating procedure\n\nWhen the requested item is a thumbnail:\n\n1. treat it as its own image task\n2. identify exact title text\n3. identify exact artist / author text\n4. identify whole-work central theme\n5. identify strongest visual metaphor\n6. design clear focal hierarchy\n7. leave intentional typography space\n8. ensure readability at small size\n9. inspect text carefully after generation\n\nA thumbnail counts as one image when the user says it does.\n\n## 18. Poster / designed image procedure\n\nWhen the requested item is a poster or designed composition:\n\n- retrieve all required text\n- retrieve any branding rules\n- retrieve any product or event facts\n- design layout space intentionally\n- ensure text is not crammed into the image as an afterthought\n- inspect both visual quality and text hierarchy\n\n## 19. Source-sensitive and fact-sensitive work\n\nIf correctness depends on outside facts, retrieve the necessary factual information before generation.\n\nExamples:\n- rankings\n- schedules\n- results\n- product specs\n- event line-ups\n- award winners\n- dates\n\nDo not guess at factual content when the image depends on it.\n\n## 20. Sensitive-content caution\n\nWhen the source contains body horror, injury, medical detail, disturbing psychological themes or anything that could be misconstrued as graphic:\n\n- translate it carefully into acceptable visual language\n- prefer implication over explicit gore unless clearly requested and allowed\n- avoid accidental graphic interpretation\n- phrase prompts so the intended emotional content is preserved without triggering unnecessary graphic output\n\nThis is especially important when surrealism is involved.\n\n## 21. Delivery and completion\n\nA Visual Studio task is only complete when:\n\n- the exact number of requested final images exists\n- each image has been checked\n- failed items have been identified or corrected\n- the user’s requested ratios and formats are met\n- thumbnails/posters are counted correctly\n- there is no false claim of completion\n\nIf the task is partial, say so clearly.\n\n## 22. Handoff to Animation Studio\n\nWhen approved images are intended for later animation, record and hand over:\n\n- approved image file / reference\n- exact source moment\n- shot purpose\n- character continuity details\n- environment and composition\n- mood / emotional beat\n- props and important objects\n- what motion seems likely or intended\n- what must not change\n\nAnimation Studio must animate from the approved image, not redesign the image from scratch.\n\n## 23. Current limitation statement\n\nThis procedure defines HOW Visual Studio should operate.\nIt does not itself guarantee:\n- a connected image generator\n- persistent project memory\n- automatic asset management\n- automatic publishing\n- automatic cross-tool compatibility\n\nThose require actual implementation.\n\n## 24. Development note\n\nThis file should be used together with:\n- the universal workflow\n- CBTeD Core\n- the image generation prompt master guide\n- Visual Studio\n\nIt is the execution manual for practical image generation.\n"},"animation/Animation_Studio.md":{"sha":"54ab8d25d2196c430940a42014db3dcf95504999","content":"# CBTeD Animation Studio\n\nVersion: 0.2 (development)\nStatus: Platform-neutral specialist studio and operational specification; not yet a deployed video-generation application.\n\n## Governing layers\n\n1. **Universal execution rules:** `core/Master_Workflow_Rules_Universal_Creative_Execution.txt`. These govern the method, sequential actions, checking, correction, and completion.\n2. **Image-to-video prompting rules:** `animation/sources/Image_to_Video_Prompt_Master_Guide.md`. These govern how to write motion prompts across image-to-video tools.\n3. **Original reference archive:** `animation/sources/Original_Image_to_Video_Guide_Archive.txt`. This is a verbatim extracted copy of the user's original three-page source, kept for provenance. Some claims in it concern the platform used when it was written; do not promote those into universal technical guarantees.\n4. **Project source:** approved input image, source lyrics/story/script, previous clip or final frame, project continuity, user corrections, exact output requirements.\n5. **Provider adapter:** current, verified tool-specific capabilities, controls and submission format. This is not part of the universal prompting technique.\n\nRead the relevant **full** sources before acting. This entrypoint does not replace the master rules or the complete prompt guide. Apply applicable safety and platform constraints.\n\n## Mission\n\nTurn approved images into correctly configured, individually generated and verified video shots. The studio can start from an image supplied by the user or accept approved images from Visual Studio. Do not require the Writing or Visual Studio stages if the user already has usable source material.\n\nThe prompt guide is only one component of production. **Uploading the image, selecting the correct mode and settings, submitting, inspecting and saving the result are separate mandatory operations** when the user asks for actual generation.\n\n## Operating procedure: one shot at a time\n\n### Phase A: retrieve and lock inputs\n\n1. Locate the actual approved image file/reference, rather than using a summary of its contents.\n2. Locate the exact lyric, script beat, scene direction or animation request and the relevant timing.\n3. Read project-level constraints and approved character, costume, prop, location and style continuity.\n4. Determine whether this is a new shot, an extension of a prior clip, or a revision of a failed shot.\n5. For an extension, inspect the **actual preceding clip and its end frame** where supported. Establish starting pose, camera momentum, environment and sound continuity.\n6. Establish the requested number of **separate** outputs and exact completion criteria. Record the current shot index and outstanding shots.\n\n### Phase B: select the tool and verify its capabilities\n\n7. Identify the user-requested image-to-video service. If none is specified, choose only among genuinely available, suitable services and explain a material difference when it affects output.\n8. Determine whether a connected tool can **actually** upload an image and generate video. Do not claim access merely because the tool's name appears in instructions.\n9. Read the tool's current accepted image formats, input limits, image-to-video mode, model options, clip duration, aspect ratios, crop modes, output resolution, audio support, extension support, reference-image support, credits/cost, and submission schema where exposed.\n10. If a needed control or capability is unavailable or unknown, **do not invent it**. Ask only for a genuinely necessary decision; otherwise make a supported default choice and disclose relevant limitations.\n11. If no suitable generation tool is connected, stop at a fully prepared, clearly labelled prompt and settings handoff. Do not claim to have uploaded or generated anything.\n\n### Phase C: prepare and attach the source image\n\n12. Verify the input asset opens and is the correct approved version. Record its filename or source ID and dimensions if available.\n13. Check whether the input image matches the requested video aspect ratio. Determine whether the selected service supports that ratio and whether it would crop, stretch, pad or reframe.\n14. Preserve the image as supplied unless the user authorises an edit or the required conversion is part of the request. Never silently crop a character or change the composition to force a ratio.\n15. Select the tool's **image-to-video** workflow, not text-to-video or image-generation mode.\n16. Upload/attach the correct image through an actually available tool action. Confirm the service accepted the asset and it is assigned to the intended start-frame/reference slot. For extensions, use the verified end frame or extension source required by that service.\n17. If upload fails, identify the failure, retain the original asset and retry only using supported actions or report the blocker.\n\n### Phase D: configure generation before prompting\n\n18. Choose the requested provider/model or a supported alternative authorised by the user.\n19. Select the **exact requested aspect ratio** in the tool if supported. Verify the setting rather than assuming it was inherited from the image. If the service determines ratio automatically, verify its documented behaviour and resulting crop.\n20. Select duration, resolution, frame rate, quality tier, seed, motion strength, camera controls, reference strength and other parameters **only when the actual tool exposes them**. Never pretend these controls exist universally.\n21. Set audio on/off, soundtrack, dialogue or Foley according to the request and the service's real capabilities. Avoid adding generated audio when the user wants a silent clip for later music editing.\n22. Check model restrictions, estimated credit cost where exposed, export format, output destination and any extension settings.\n23. Re-read the configured values and compare them with the current shot specification before submission. Resolve mismatches first.\n\n### Phase E: write the image-to-video prompt\n\n24. Consult the full `Image_to_Video_Prompt_Master_Guide.md`.\n25. Study the **actual input image** and write the temporal change, rather than redescribing static features already visible.\n26. Define the immediate action, progression, meaningful subject kinetics, purposeful camera trajectory, relevant atmospheric changes and the intended end state.\n27. Include sound directions when requested and supported; use the guide's `AUDIO:` convention or a provider-compatible equivalent.\n28. For music, synchronise the visual arc with the exact approved passage and intended duration; do not invent lyrics or dialogue.\n29. For an extension, continue from the **verified** prior end state. Use continuity-lock phrasing where useful, without assuming that words alone enforce consistency.\n30. Respect the service's actual prompt length, field types, supported camera controls and syntax. Provider-specific differences belong in an adapter; do not rewrite the platform-neutral creative principles as though a provider-specific interface were universal.\n31. Verify that the prompt contains only desired action and relevant motion, follows chronology, is physically coherent with the source image, and respects all project corrections.\n\n### Phase F: submit one generation\n\n32. Recheck source image attachment, correct workflow mode, selected model, aspect ratio/crop, duration, quality, audio, prompt, cost and target output count.\n33. Submit **one authorised shot/segment** through the real generation action.\n34. Record the returned job ID or equivalent only if supplied. Do not fabricate one.\n35. Wait for the provider's actual completion/status mechanism. Do not assume that submission means successful generation.\n36. If the provider rejects or fails the job, preserve successful earlier clips, record the exact error and correct only the failed step. Do not silently submit an unrelated replacement.\n\n### Phase G: inspect and record the output\n\n37. Retrieve/open the generated video when supported. Check that it is a playable video, not merely a prompt, preview image or queued job.\n38. Verify actual orientation/aspect ratio, dimensions, duration and presence/absence of audio when this information is accessible.\n39. Inspect source fidelity, character and object identity, anatomy, action, camera path, motion continuity, end frame, sound and prohibited changes.\n40. Compare the result with the exact shot's source and project rules. Distinguish objective failure from optional artistic preference.\n41. Preserve the generated file or returned provider reference in the actual accessible project state. Do not claim a file is saved or persistent unless verified.\n42. Record the starting image ID, shot number, prompt version, settings actually used, output reference, verified final state, success/failure and remaining shot count.\n43. If the clip is rejected or noncompliant, revise the relevant input, setting or prompt and regenerate **that** clip; retain previously accepted clips.\n\n### Phase H: advance or hand off\n\n44. For a requested extension, use the verified prior clip/end frame and repeat the full preparation, configuration, prompting, generation and checking cycle.\n45. For a distinct new shot, retrieve its approved source image and exact narrative moment, and repeat the cycle independently.\n46. Do not report the overall task complete until the requested number of separate clips has been generated and verified, or identify precisely what remains blocked.\n47. Hand off the actual output references and shot order for assembly/editing. A proposed sequence or unexecuted prompt is not a rendered video.\n\n## Multi-segment storytelling\n\nThe source guide illustrates a three-part narrative arc: establishment, development and resolution. This is a **creative structure**, not a universal ten-second duration setting. Adapt segment count and length to the user's request and the chosen service's verified constraints.\n\nEach segment starts from an approved image or a verified preceding endpoint. Each is independently submitted and checked. Never merge multiple requested generations into one call just to save steps.\n\n## Provider-specific adapters\n\nStore service-specific operating instructions separately from this file. Each adapter must document the **actual current** supported upload method, workflow mode, start/end-frame handling, ratio and crop options, duration/resolution controls, audio controls, prompt limits, generation action, polling/status, export and failure handling. Mark unknown or unavailable controls as such.\n\nA provider's UI may change. Do not claim to click a UI control unless a connected action actually allows it; if necessary, provide exact user-facing steps based on verified interface details. Never infer an API capability from the appearance of a consumer web interface.\n\n## Studio independence and shared project state\n\nWriting Studio provides approved narrative source when used. Visual Studio provides approved images and reference continuity when used. Animation Studio retrieves and inspects the **actual assets** and may operate alone with user-supplied material.\n\nShare approved project decisions, source identifiers, completed and failed shot IDs, current task position and handoff data only through storage or context that is actually accessible. Do not claim that this Markdown file implements a persistent database.\n\n## Completion status\n\nThis is a detailed **operational instruction specification**, not an installed image-to-video engine. A functioning implementation still requires connected generation actions, provider adapters, shared project state and the necessary authorisations. The prompt guide and universal workflow remain separate, authoritative source documents.\n"},"animation/sources/Image_to_Video_Prompt_Master_Guide.md":{"sha":"48bf97cc3de502235643e17fbb715802809e14c0","content":"# Image-to-Video Prompt Master Guide\n\n## Scope and provenance\nThis is the **platform-neutral working adaptation** of the user's three-page image-to-video prompting guide. It retains the creative method: temporal change rather than static-image description, four motion vectors, chronological action, purposeful camera direction, audio direction, and continuity across extensions.\n\nThe original uploaded document includes some platform-specific technical claims, model names, and fixed ten-second segment examples. Those are **not universal engine guarantees**. This adaptation does not overwrite the original source; it distinguishes general prompting technique from capabilities that must be checked in the selected tool.\n\n## Core principle: describe temporal change\nAn approved input image already establishes the static appearance, composition and environment. Prompt the **motion delta**: what starts moving, what changes, where the camera travels, how the atmosphere develops, and the final state.\n\nUse the image as visual authority. Preserve identity, geometry and style through the supplied image and tool settings where supported. Do not assume any engine will preserve these perfectly.\n\n## Four coordinated motion vectors\n**Subject kinetics:** body movement, weight shifts, gestures, eye direction, expression, interaction, physical momentum.\n\n**Camera trajectory:** static, push, pull, tracking, orbital drift, crane/pedestal or other meaningful movement. Describe direction, speed, timing and subject relationship.\n\n**Atmospheric dynamics:** changes in wind, hair/fabric movement, shadows, light, particles, weather and environmental motion when appropriate.\n\n**Acoustic direction:** synchronised Foley, ambience, speech or music only when requested and supported. An explicit `AUDIO:` section is the default writing convention for sound-capable tools. For silent tools, retain sound notes separately for editing rather than assuming the generator will render audio.\n\n## Chronological prompt construction\nBegin with the action that must occur immediately. Describe the evolving middle of the shot and finish with the intended end pose, camera state and atmosphere. Keep motion physically coherent with the input image. Do not repeatedly describe static wardrobe, faces, colours or props already visible unless a specific correction or provider limitation makes that necessary.\n\n## Camera and story\nSelect a purposeful camera path. A slow push-in can build intimacy or tension; an orbit reveals depth and relationships; tracking follows action; a locked-off shot allows the subject to dominate; a rise or crane movement changes scale and perspective. These are examples, not required effects.\n\n## Optional extended sequence\nThe source guide illustrates a **three-segment, approximately thirty-second arc**, with an establishing dynamic, a developing shift and a resolution. This is a narrative pattern, not a universal duration requirement.\n\nFor a multi-segment task:\n- Segment one: initial action, camera path, environment and sound direction.\n- Segment two: continue from the **actual verified end frame/pose** of segment one; evolve action, camera and sound while preserving continuity.\n- Segment three: continue from the **actual verified end frame/pose** of segment two; resolve movement, framing, atmosphere and sound.\n\nUse the original guide's phrase `LOCK CONSISTENCY: Continue seamlessly from ending pose.` as a prompting technique when appropriate, while recognising that text alone cannot guarantee continuity.\n\n## Prompt template for a single shot\n```text\nIMMEDIATE ACTION: [specific motion beginning in the first moments].\nDEVELOPMENT: [how the subject's motion, emotion and interaction progress].\nCAMERA: [purposeful movement, direction, speed and framing progression].\nENVIRONMENT: [relevant evolving lighting, weather, fabric, particles or shadows].\nEND STATE: [precise final action, pose, camera framing and motion].\nAUDIO: [desired synchronised sounds/dialogue/ambience, if supported and requested].\n```\n\nThe actual submission format may be plain prose if the chosen engine performs better with prose. Do not confuse a convenient template with a verified provider API requirement.\n\n## Production settings are a separate responsibility\nThe prompting guide **does not** establish universal model menus, aspect-ratio selectors, clip lengths, audio controls, resolution options, upload interfaces, seed settings, extension endpoints or export procedures. These must be handled by a separate **image-to-video operating procedure** that checks the current tool and its supported options.\n\nBefore submission verify the approved image, correct image-to-video mode, selected provider/model, aspect ratio, crop behaviour, duration, output resolution, audio settings, reference images, camera controls, cost/credits and destination. After generation verify the actual returned clip, continuity and output dimensions. Do not invent controls absent from the chosen service.\n\n## Boundaries\nThe same creative method applies across image-to-video tools, but exact syntax and engine features vary. Provider-specific details belong in separately maintained adapters, never in this general guide. The source guide is a prompting method, not a complete executable tool-operation manual.\n"}};
const SUPABASE_URL = (Deno.env.get("SUPABASE_URL") || "https://dmzzyfiwtosxtyekpbfi.supabase.co").replace(/\/$/, "");
const RESOURCE = SUPABASE_URL + "/functions/v1/cbted-mcp";
const METADATA = RESOURCE + "/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource";
const AUTH_ISSUER = SUPABASE_URL + "/auth/v1";
const headers = {"Content-Type":"application/json; charset=utf-8","Cache-Control":"no-store","Access-Control-Allow-Origin":"*","Access-Control-Allow-Headers":"Content-Type, Authorization, Accept, Mcp-Session-Id, Mcp-Protocol-Version","Access-Control-Allow-Methods":"GET, POST, OPTIONS","Vary":"Origin"};
const uuidRe = /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[1-8][0-9a-f]{3}-[89ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i;
const tool = (name,description,properties,required=[],readOnly=true,publicAccess=false) => ({
  name, description,
  inputSchema:{type:"object",properties,required,additionalProperties:false},
  annotations:{readOnlyHint:readOnly,destructiveHint:false,openWorldHint:false},
  securitySchemes:publicAccess?[{type:"noauth"}]:[{type:"oauth2",scopes:[]}]
});
const S={type:"string"}; const U={type:"string",format:"uuid"};
const tools = [
  tool("cbted_get_rulebook","FIRST for any CBTeD creative work. Return the COMPLETE authoritative universal master workflow and the chosen studio procedure with content and source SHA. This server supplies rules, while ChatGPT itself uses its available native writing/image/video tools one operation at a time. Do not claim an action occurred until its native tool result is verified.",{studio:{type:"string",enum:["core","writing","visual","animation"],description:"Studio whose complete instructions should accompany universal rules."}},["studio"],true,true),
  tool("cbted_list_projects","List projects belonging to the authenticated user, preserving project identity and continuity.",{},[],true),
  tool("cbted_get_project_context","Read an owned project, rules, source metadata, approvals, studio settings, assets, tasks, outputs and handoffs. For exact writing content call cbted_get_source_version after selecting the version.",{project_id:U},["project_id"],true),
  tool("cbted_get_source_version","Retrieve the actual complete source-version text, ideas and suggestions without substituting a summary.",{project_id:U,source_version_id:U},["project_id","source_version_id"],true),
  tool("cbted_create_project","Create a user-owned persistent creative project; this does not begin generation.",{title:{type:"string",minLength:1,maxLength:200},description:S},["title"],false),
  tool("cbted_create_source","Register a titled song, story, poem, script, treatment or other source within an owned project before adding versions.",{project_id:U,title:{type:"string",minLength:1,maxLength:200},source_kind:S},["project_id","title"],false),
  tool("cbted_save_writing_version","Append a new numbered version of an existing source, preserving previous drafts. Keep creator writing, ideas and suggestions in separate fields. Never silently approve this version.",{project_id:U,source_id:U,content:S,ideas:S,suggested_text:S,change_note:S},["project_id","source_id"],false),
  tool("cbted_approve_source_version","Record an explicit user decision about a particular source version. Approval is append-only and must not be invented or inferred.",{project_id:U,source_version_id:U,decision:{type:"string",enum:["approved","rejected","revoked"]},note:S},["project_id","source_version_id","decision"],false),
  tool("cbted_add_project_rule","Append a locked project or studio rule/correction, retaining previous revisions. Only use for a user-authorised decision.",{project_id:U,scope:{type:"string",enum:["project","writing","visual","animation"]},rule_key:{type:"string",minLength:1,maxLength:160},rule_kind:{type:"string",enum:["constraint","correction","decision","preference","reference"]},body:{type:"string",minLength:1},is_locked:{type:"boolean"}},["project_id","scope","rule_key","rule_kind","body"],false),
  tool("cbted_create_task","Register ONE creative operation with count and requirements. This does not generate content or mark outputs complete; ChatGPT must separately invoke a real native creative tool.",{project_id:U,studio:{type:"string",enum:["writing","visual","animation"]},operation:S,requested_units:{type:"integer",minimum:1,maximum:50},source_version_id:U,start_asset_id:U,requirements:{type:"object"},current_step:S},["project_id","studio","operation","requested_units"],false),
  tool("cbted_record_output","After an actual ChatGPT creative tool result, record ONE output. Text requires its actual content; image/video/audio requires a real persisted project asset ID. A temporary ChatGPT sandbox reference is not proof of durable storage. Verification is pending unless evidence is explicitly supplied.",{project_id:U,task_id:U,output_kind:{type:"string",enum:["text","image","video","audio","prompt","document","other"]},sequence_index:{type:"integer",minimum:1},content:S,asset_id:U,source_version_id:U,verification_status:{type:"string",enum:["pending","passed","needs_repair","failed"]},verification_note:S},["project_id","task_id","output_kind"],false),
  tool("cbted_set_task_state","Update the current step/status of a task. Completion requires every requested output to have passed verification and to exist as records. Do not use this to pretend generation occurred.",{project_id:U,task_id:U,status:{type:"string",enum:["pending","in_progress","blocked","completed","failed","cancelled"]},current_step:S},["project_id","task_id","status"],false),
  tool("cbted_get_asset_link","For an asset owned by the connected user, create a short-lived signed URL to access its PRIVATE Supabase Storage file. ChatGPT must still inspect the actual source image rather than trusting its metadata.",{project_id:U,asset_id:U},["project_id","asset_id"],true)
];
const isUUID = (v) => typeof v==="string" && uuidRe.test(v);
const requireUUID=(v,k)=>{if(!isUUID(v))throw Error("Invalid "+k);return v;};
const requiredText=(v,k,max=100000)=>{if(typeof v!=="string"||!v.trim()||v.length>max)throw Error("Invalid "+k);return v;};
const allowedText=(v,max=100000)=>{if(v===undefined||v===null)return null;if(typeof v!=="string"||v.length>max)throw Error("Text exceeds allowed length");return v;};
const ownedProject=async (token,id)=>{
 requireUUID(id,"project_id");
 const rows=await rest(token,"projects",{id:"eq."+id,select:"id,title,description,status,current_studio,created_at,updated_at",limit:"1"});
 if(!rows.length)throw Error("Project not found or not accessible");
 return rows[0];
};
function publishableKey(){
 const raw=Deno.env.get("SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEYS");
 if(raw){try{const k=JSON.parse(raw);if(k.default)return k.default;}catch(_e){}}
 return Deno.env.get("SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY") || Deno.env.get("SUPABASE_ANON_KEY") || "";
}
async function authenticate(request){
 const match=/^Bearer ([^\s]+)$/.exec(request.headers.get("Authorization")||"");
 if(!match)return null;
 const key=publishableKey();if(!key)throw Error("Server publishable key not configured");
 const response=await fetch(SUPABASE_URL+"/auth/v1/user",{headers:{"apikey":key,"Authorization":"Bearer "+match[1]}});
 if(!response.ok)return null;
 const user=await response.json();
 if(!isUUID(user.id))return null;
 return {id:user.id,email:user.email||null,token:match[1]};
}
function challenge(){
 return 'Bearer resource_metadata="'+METADATA+'", error="invalid_token", error_description="Connect your CBTeD account to access private projects"';
}
function authError(){
 return {isError:true,content:[{type:"text",text:"CBTeD authentication required. Connect your existing CBTeD Supabase account."}],_meta:{"mcp/www_authenticate":[challenge()]}};
}
async function rest(token,table,query={},method="GET",body=null){
 const allowed=["projects","sources","source_versions","project_rules","assets","tasks","outputs","approvals","handoffs","studio_settings","project_runs"];
 if(!allowed.includes(table))throw Error("Unsupported table");
 const qs=new URLSearchParams(query);
 const url=SUPABASE_URL+"/rest/v1/"+table+(qs.toString()?"?"+qs:"");
 const request={method,headers:{"apikey":publishableKey(),"Authorization":"Bearer "+token,"Content-Type":"application/json","Prefer":"return=representation"}};
 if(body!==null)request.body=JSON.stringify(body);
 const response=await fetch(url,request);
 const text=await response.text();let data;
 try{data=text?JSON.parse(text):null;}catch(_e){throw Error("Database returned an invalid response");}
 if(!response.ok)throw Error("Database rejected request ("+response.status+"): "+String(data?.message||data?.error||"Access denied").slice(0,250));
 return data;
}
const byProject=(id,select="*")=>({project_id:"eq."+id,select,limit:"50"});
async function runTool(name,a,token,user){
 if(name==="cbted_get_rulebook"){
   const studio=a.studio||"core";
   if(!["core","writing","visual","animation"].includes(studio))throw Error("Unknown studio");
   const paths=["core/Master_Workflow_Rules_Universal_Creative_Execution.txt","core/CBTeD_Core.md"];
   if(studio==="writing")paths.push("writing/Writing_Studio_Operating_Procedure.md");
   if(studio==="visual")paths.push("visual/Visual_Studio_Operating_Procedure.md");
   if(studio==="animation")paths.push("animation/Animation_Studio.md","animation/sources/Image_to_Video_Prompt_Master_Guide.md");
   return {studio,source:"Pinned verbatim GitHub source snapshot",documents:paths.map(path=>({path,sha:RULEBOOK[path].sha,content:RULEBOOK[path].content})),execution_role:"ChatGPT performs creative operations using its own available native tools. MCP only supplies instructions and project state. Follow the full authoritative rules; no shortcut to a prompt-only substitute."};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_list_projects"){
   return {projects:await rest(token,"projects",{select:"id,title,description,status,current_studio,created_at,updated_at",order:"updated_at.desc",limit:"50"})};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_create_project"){
   const title=requiredText(a.title,"title",200);
   const row={owner_id:user.id,title,description:allowedText(a.description,5000)};
   return {project:(await rest(token,"projects",{}, "POST",row))[0]};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_get_project_context"){
   const p=await ownedProject(token,a.project_id);
   const pid=p.id;
   const [rules,sources,versions,approvals,settings,assets,tasks,outputs,handoffs]=await Promise.all([
     rest(token,"project_rules",{...byProject(pid),order:"created_at.desc",limit:"100"}),
     rest(token,"sources",{...byProject(pid),order:"created_at.desc"}),
     rest(token,"source_versions",{...byProject(pid,"id,project_id,source_id,version_number,based_on_id,change_note,created_at"),order:"created_at.desc",limit:"100"}),
     rest(token,"approvals",{...byProject(pid),order:"created_at.desc",limit:"100"}),
     rest(token,"studio_settings",{...byProject(pid),order:"created_at.desc",limit:"50"}),
     rest(token,"assets",{...byProject(pid),order:"created_at.desc",limit:"100"}),
     rest(token,"tasks",{...byProject(pid),order:"updated_at.desc",limit:"100"}),
     rest(token,"outputs",{...byProject(pid,"id,project_id,task_id,output_kind,sequence_index,asset_id,source_version_id,verification_status,details,created_at"),order:"created_at.desc",limit:"100"}),
     rest(token,"handoffs",{...byProject(pid),order:"created_at.desc",limit:"50"})
   ]);
   return {project:p,rules,sources,source_versions:versions,approvals,studio_settings:settings,assets,tasks,outputs,handoffs,note:"Read exact writing with cbted_get_source_version. Read full master rules using cbted_get_rulebook. Output metadata alone is not evidence of generated media."};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_get_source_version"){
   await ownedProject(token,a.project_id);requireUUID(a.source_version_id,"source_version_id");
   const rows=await rest(token,"source_versions",{...byProject(a.project_id),id:"eq."+a.source_version_id,limit:"1"});
   if(!rows.length)throw Error("Version not found or access denied");
   return {source_version:rows[0]};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_create_source"){
   await ownedProject(token,a.project_id);
   return {source:(await rest(token,"sources",{},"POST",{project_id:a.project_id,title:requiredText(a.title,"title",200),source_kind:allowedText(a.source_kind,60)||"other"}))[0]};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_save_writing_version"){
   await ownedProject(token,a.project_id);requireUUID(a.source_id,"source_id");
   const sources=await rest(token,"sources",{...byProject(a.project_id),id:"eq."+a.source_id,limit:"1"});
   if(!sources.length)throw Error("Source not found or access denied");
   const prior=await rest(token,"source_versions",{...byProject(a.project_id,"id,version_number"),source_id:"eq."+a.source_id,order:"version_number.desc",limit:"1"});
   const content=allowedText(a.content,250000),ideas=allowedText(a.ideas,250000),suggested=allowedText(a.suggested_text,250000);
   if(![content,ideas,suggested].some(v=>v?.trim()))throw Error("Provide source content, ideas or suggestions");
   const record={project_id:a.project_id,source_id:a.source_id,version_number:(prior[0]?.version_number||0)+1,content,ideas,suggested_text:suggested,based_on_id:prior[0]?.id||null,change_note:allowedText(a.change_note,5000)};
   return {source_version:(await rest(token,"source_versions",{},"POST",record))[0],note:"Draft saved. Approval has not been assumed."};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_approve_source_version"){
   await ownedProject(token,a.project_id);requireUUID(a.source_version_id,"source_version_id");
   if(!["approved","rejected","revoked"].includes(a.decision))throw Error("Invalid decision");
   const versions=await rest(token,"source_versions",{...byProject(a.project_id,"id"),id:"eq."+a.source_version_id,limit:"1"});
   if(!versions.length)throw Error("Source version not found");
   return {approval:(await rest(token,"approvals",{},"POST",{project_id:a.project_id,source_version_id:a.source_version_id,decision:a.decision,decided_by:user.id,note:allowedText(a.note,5000)}))[0]};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_add_project_rule"){
   await ownedProject(token,a.project_id);
   if(!["project","writing","visual","animation"].includes(a.scope)||!["constraint","correction","decision","preference","reference"].includes(a.rule_kind))throw Error("Invalid scope or rule kind");
   const key=requiredText(a.rule_key,"rule_key",160);
   const prior=await rest(token,"project_rules",{...byProject(a.project_id,"id,revision_number"),scope:"eq."+a.scope,rule_key:"eq."+key,order:"revision_number.desc",limit:"1"});
   const row={project_id:a.project_id,scope:a.scope,rule_key:key,rule_kind:a.rule_kind,body:requiredText(a.body,"body",20000),is_locked:a.is_locked!==false,revision_number:(prior[0]?.revision_number||0)+1,supersedes_id:prior[0]?.id||null};
   return {rule:(await rest(token,"project_rules",{},"POST",row))[0]};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_create_task"){
   await ownedProject(token,a.project_id);
   if(!["writing","visual","animation"].includes(a.studio))throw Error("Invalid studio");
   const n=a.requested_units;if(!Number.isInteger(n)||n<1||n>50)throw Error("Invalid requested output count");
   const row={project_id:a.project_id,studio:a.studio,operation:requiredText(a.operation,"operation",1000),requested_units:n,completed_units:0,status:"pending",current_step:allowedText(a.current_step,5000)||"Retrieve source and rules; verify before each native creative action",requirements:a.requirements||{}};
   if(a.source_version_id)row.source_version_id=requireUUID(a.source_version_id,"source_version_id");
   if(a.start_asset_id)row.start_asset_id=requireUUID(a.start_asset_id,"start_asset_id");
   return {task:(await rest(token,"tasks",{},"POST",row))[0],executed:false,note:"Task recorded. No creative output has been generated."};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_record_output"){
   await ownedProject(token,a.project_id);requireUUID(a.task_id,"task_id");
   const tasks=await rest(token,"tasks",{...byProject(a.project_id),id:"eq."+a.task_id,limit:"1"});
   if(!tasks.length)throw Error("Task not found");
   const kinds=["text","image","video","audio","prompt","document","other"];
   if(!kinds.includes(a.output_kind))throw Error("Unsupported output kind");
   const row={project_id:a.project_id,task_id:a.task_id,output_kind:a.output_kind,verification_status:a.verification_status||"pending",details:{}};
   if(!["pending","passed","needs_repair","failed"].includes(row.verification_status))throw Error("Invalid verification status");
   if(a.sequence_index!==undefined){if(!Number.isInteger(a.sequence_index)||a.sequence_index<1||a.sequence_index>tasks[0].requested_units)throw Error("Invalid sequence index");row.sequence_index=a.sequence_index;}
   if(a.source_version_id)row.source_version_id=requireUUID(a.source_version_id,"source_version_id");
   if(a.asset_id){
     requireUUID(a.asset_id,"asset_id");
     const assets=await rest(token,"assets",{...byProject(a.project_id,"id"),id:"eq."+a.asset_id,limit:"1"});
     if(!assets.length)throw Error("Project asset not found");
     row.asset_id=a.asset_id;
   }
   if(["image","video","audio"].includes(a.output_kind)&&!row.asset_id)throw Error("Media outputs need an actually stored private project asset ID; a temporary ChatGPT file or generated prompt is not persistent.");
   if(a.content)row.content=allowedText(a.content,250000);
   if(!row.asset_id&&!row.content?.trim())throw Error("An actual output content or project asset ID is required");
   if(a.verification_note)row.details.verification_note=allowedText(a.verification_note,5000);
   if(row.verification_status==="passed"&&!row.details.verification_note)throw Error("Verification evidence/inspection note required before marking an output passed");
   return {output:(await rest(token,"outputs",{},"POST",row))[0],note:"Output recorded; no media-generation claim is made by this server."};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_set_task_state"){
   await ownedProject(token,a.project_id);requireUUID(a.task_id,"task_id");
   const taskRows=await rest(token,"tasks",{...byProject(a.project_id),id:"eq."+a.task_id,limit:"1"});
   if(!taskRows.length)throw Error("Task not found");
   const task=taskRows[0],statuses=["pending","in_progress","blocked","completed","failed","cancelled"];
   if(!statuses.includes(a.status))throw Error("Invalid status");
   const passed=await rest(token,"outputs",{...byProject(a.project_id,"id,sequence_index"),task_id:"eq."+a.task_id,verification_status:"eq.passed",limit:"100"});
   const verified=passed.filter(o=>o.sequence_index!==null);
   const completedUnits=new Set(verified.map(o=>o.sequence_index)).size;
   if(a.status==="completed"&&completedUnits!==task.requested_units)throw Error("All requested individual outputs must be recorded and verified before completing task");
   const patch={status:a.status,completed_units:Math.min(task.requested_units,completedUnits),current_step:allowedText(a.current_step,5000)||task.current_step};
   return {task:(await rest(token,"tasks",{id:"eq."+a.task_id,project_id:"eq."+a.project_id},"PATCH",patch))[0]};
 }
 if(name==="cbted_get_asset_link"){
   await ownedProject(token,a.project_id);requireUUID(a.asset_id,"asset_id");
   const rows=await rest(token,"assets",{...byProject(a.project_id),id:"eq."+a.asset_id,limit:"1"});
   if(!rows.length)throw Error("Asset not found");
   const asset=rows[0],parts=asset.storage_path.split("/").map(encodeURIComponent);
   const signed=await fetch(SUPABASE_URL+"/storage/v1/object/sign/cbted-project-assets/"+parts.join("/"),{method:"POST",headers:{"apikey":publishableKey(),"Authorization":"Bearer "+token,"Content-Type":"application/json"},body:JSON.stringify({expiresIn:120})});
   if(!signed.ok)throw Error("Private storage rejected signed link request");
   const data=await signed.json();
   const relative=data.signedURL||data.signedUrl;
   if(!relative)throw Error("Private storage did not return a signed URL");
   return {asset,temporary_url:relative.startsWith("http")?relative:SUPABASE_URL+"/storage/v1"+(relative.startsWith("/")?relative:"/"+relative),expires_in_seconds:120,note:"Temporary read-only access. Inspect actual asset before creative use."};
 }
 throw Error("Unknown tool name");
}
function reply(id,result){return {jsonrpc:"2.0",id,result};}
function fail(id,code,message){return {jsonrpc:"2.0",id,error:{code,message}};}
function response(value,status=200,extra={}){return new Response(JSON.stringify(value),{status,headers:{...headers,...extra}});}
Deno.serve(async(request)=>{
 if(request.method==="OPTIONS")return new Response(null,{status:204,headers});
 const path=new URL(request.url).pathname;
 if(request.method==="GET"){
   if(path.endsWith("/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource"))return response({resource:RESOURCE,authorization_servers:[AUTH_ISSUER],scopes_supported:["openid","email"],resource_documentation:"https://github.com/CannaBusTeD/CBTeD"});
   if(path.endsWith("/cbted-mcp")||path.endsWith("/cbted-mcp/"))return response({name:"CBTeD MCP",status:"online",version:"0.1.0",authentication:"OAuth/Supabase bearer required for private tools",creative_generation:"Runs in ChatGPT, not in MCP server",rule_documents:Object.keys(RULEBOOK).length});
   return response({error:"Not found"},404);
 }
 if(request.method!=="POST")return response({error:"Method not allowed"},405);
 if(!path.endsWith("/cbted-mcp")&&!path.endsWith("/cbted-mcp/"))return response({error:"Not found"},404);
 if(Number(request.headers.get("content-length")||0)>500000)return response({error:"Request too large"},413);
 let rpc;try{rpc=await request.json();}catch(_e){return response(fail(null,-32700,"Invalid JSON"),400);}
 if(!rpc||typeof rpc!=="object"||Array.isArray(rpc))return response(fail(null,-32600,"Invalid request"),400);
 if(rpc.method==="notifications/initialized")return new Response(null,{status:202,headers});
 if(rpc.method==="initialize"){
   const versions=["2025-11-25","2025-06-18","2025-03-26","2024-11-05"];
   const asked=rpc.params?.protocolVersion;
   return response(reply(rpc.id,{protocolVersion:versions.includes(asked)?asked:"2025-06-18",capabilities:{tools:{listChanged:false}},serverInfo:{name:"CBTeD",version:"0.1.0"},instructions:"Call cbted_get_rulebook first for the full authoritative rules before any creative work. Use ChatGPT native tools to perform each requested output independently; MCP is only the project memory/rules bridge. Never claim an output exists from a task record alone."}));
 }
 if(rpc.method==="ping")return response(reply(rpc.id,{}));
 if(rpc.method==="tools/list")return response(reply(rpc.id,{tools}));
 if(rpc.method==="tools/call"){
   const name=rpc.params?.name;
   if(!tools.some(t=>t.name===name))return response(fail(rpc.id,-32601,"Unknown tool"));
   const args=rpc.params?.arguments||{};
   if(typeof args!=="object"||Array.isArray(args))return response(fail(rpc.id,-32602,"Invalid arguments"));
   try{
     let identity=null;
     if(name!=="cbted_get_rulebook"){
       identity=await authenticate(request);
       if(!identity)return response(reply(rpc.id,authError()));
     }
     const result=await runTool(name,args,identity?.token||"",identity);
     return response(reply(rpc.id,{content:[{type:"text",text:JSON.stringify(result)}],structuredContent:result,isError:false}));
   }catch(e){return response(reply(rpc.id,{content:[{type:"text",text:String(e?.message||"Unexpected error").slice(0,600)}],isError:true}));}
 }
 return response(fail(rpc.id??null,-32601,"Unsupported method"));
});
